    "origin_id": "https://www.autoevolution.com/news/cadillac-xt5-is-off-to-a-good-start-and-this-is-what-america-is-missing-277589.html",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.autoevolution.com/news/cadillac-xt5-is-off-to-a-good-start-and-this-is-what-america-is-missing-277589.html",
    "origin_publisher": "autoevolution",
    "published_at": "2026-10-26T22:00:01+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-10-26T22:00:01+0000",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-10-26T22:25:07.637362+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-10-26T22:25:07.637362+00:00",
    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • OMODA 4 Lands in Paris To Showcase Futuristic Styling, Looks Like a Discount Urus\nCadillac XT5 Is Off to a Good Start and This Is What America Is Missing\n10 photos\nPhoto: 2027 Cadillac XT5 PHEV\nThe 2027 Cadillac XT5 PHEV is off to a strong start in China. The new variant racked up over 3,000 orders in just its first 17 days on the market after it was officially launched in early September. The XT5 is also available in the United States, but it is a completely different vehicle.\nOffered in Air, Pro, and Pro Max trims, with higher-end trims launching first and the entry-level Air debuting in October, the Cadillac XT5 PHEV starts at 239,900 yuan, which translates to roughly $35,900. The U.S. XT5 is a $44,800 deal.\nThe Chinese Cadillac XT5 PHEV uses local tech, featuring the Momenta R7 driver-assistance system with a LiDAR sensor present on the higher trims. The plug-in variant pairs a 1.5-liter turbocharged inline-four engine with a dual-motor setup, operating in an all-wheel-drive layout.\nA two-speed Direct Hybrid Transmission puts the power down through all four corners. The system pumps out 362 horsepower (367 metric horsepower) and 369 pound-feet (500 Newton meters) of torque for a run from 0 to 60 mph (0 to 97 kph) in 4.6 seconds on its way to a top speed of 130 mph (210 kph).\nPhoto: Cadillac China The Cadillac XT5 PHEV is equipped with a 35.5 kWh ternary lithium battery pack, which stores enough energy for a drive of 134 miles (215 kilometers) on electric power only under the very generous Chinese testing cycle.\nThe car features a highly advanced 5C charge rating, allowing it to charge from 30% to 80% in just 15 minutes using a compatible DC fast charger, and supports an external discharge capacity of up to 6 kW, meaning it can act as a mobile generator to power appliances, camping gear, or tools.\nGeneral Motors has no current plans to bring the China-engineered XT5 PHEV to North America, where the aging XT5 remains, even though it is sitting on an outdated platform. The XT5 made its debut on the U.S. market back in 2016 as a 2017 model year, which basically makes it a decade old with only minor updates.\nThe XT5 is thus one of the oldest luxury vehicles still on sale today. While Cadillac is selling an EREV and a PHEV in China, the American XT5 relies strictly on non-electrified combustion engines.\nPhoto: Cadillac USA The 2.0-liter turbocharged four-cylinder unit sits at the base of the lineup with 235 horsepower (238 metric horsepower) and 258 pound-feet (350 Newton meters) of torque. In the meantime, the 3.6-liter naturally aspirated V6 has to deal with 310 horsepower (314 metric horsepower) and 271 pound-feet (367 Newton meters) of torque.\nThere will not be a 2027 model-year XT5 in the US. Cadillac is completely pausing the nameplate for about nine months. In the meantime, General Motors will invest $275 million into its Spring Hill, Tennessee assembly plant, where the XT5 currently rolls off the production line. GM has reversed earlier plans to phase out the model and is reportedly planning a major next-generation update for North America.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\n2027 Cadillac XT5 PHEV Cadillac Cadillac China Cadillac XT5 PHEV price Cadillac USA\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT Jay Leno Drives Jerry O'Connell's 1979 Cadillac Seville, Stolen From His Driveway and Recovered",
